From 4b570d391c5f824532c3e8b6ee6de6c5a09d4462 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 00:35:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 14/15] Omarchy flavor: release the tunnel reset when an idle
 power-down ends, so a system resume can power the router's domain on

genpd powers every domain with an attached device on in the noirq phase
of a system resume, whatever the device's runtime PM state, and queues
the power-off again afterwards. The router's platform device stays
attached to gcc_usb4_0_gdsc while the router is powered down for sleep,
so that power-on happens without this driver.

The idle power-down left GCC_PCIE_0_TUNNEL_BCR asserted until the next
start released it. gcc_usb4_0_gdsc does not power up while that reset
is asserted, so every resume stuck the domain ("status stuck at
'off'"), the power-off after it stuck too ("stuck at 'on'"), and the
next start of the router failed with -16.

The idle power-down now releases the tunnel reset once the PCIe
preparation is retired, before its runtime vote goes and the domain can
go off. The start keeps its own call for what an Eject still holds.

Hardware (Yoga Slim 7x, 9 October 2026). Without this change the
pm_test levels freezer and devices passed, and platform, the first
with the noirq phases, stuck the domain on the way back (boot
2a77a7cf). With it, five pm_test levels and real deep and s2idle
sleeps passed on two sessions of the router, a suspend through systemd
brought the router back by itself, and a drive connected afterwards.
genpd counted 13 power-offs of the domain and refused none (boot
06d4e5e7).
---
 drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-x1-host.c |  7 +++++++
 drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-x1-pcie.c | 14 ++++++++------
 2 files changed, 15 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-x1-host.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-x1-host.c
index b586772..fa64a8f 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-x1-host.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-x1-host.c
@@ -2504,6 +2504,13 @@ static ssize_t idle_retire_once_store(struct device *dev,
 	host->idle_retire_stage = "platform-retire";
 	ret = qcom_usb4_x1_idle_retire_platform(dev, &host->pcie,
 						x1_idle_platform_owner_stopped, host);
+	if (ret)
+		goto done;
+	/* A system resume powers gcc_usb4_0_gdsc on without this driver, so the
+	 * tunnel BCR is not left held once the router's domain may go off.
+	 */
+	host->idle_retire_stage = "tunnel-reset-release";
+	ret = qcom_usb4_x1_release_retired_reset(dev);
 	if (ret)
 		goto done;
 	devm_kfree(host->dev, nhi->tx_rings);
diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-x1-pcie.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-x1-pcie.c
index a9553d1..b833b72 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-x1-pcie.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-x1-pcie.c
@@ -736,10 +736,12 @@ static int x1_pcie_release_retired_reset(struct x1_pcie_context *ctx)
 
 /* gcc_usb4_0_gdsc does not power up while the tunnel BCR is asserted: held,
  * the router's domain stays "stuck at 'off'"; released first, the same
- * power-on works (Yoga Slim 7x, 8 October 2026). A retirement holds it, so the
- * router's next start releases it here, before it asks for its domain. Only
- * the reset provider is touched. The lease is put again, which never asserts;
- * the next preparation takes its own. Caller holds owner/lifecycle exclusion.
+ * power-on works (Yoga Slim 7x, 8 October 2026). A retirement holds it. The
+ * idle retirement releases it here once its context is gone, because a system
+ * resume powers the domain on by itself; the router's start releases what an
+ * Eject still holds, before it asks for its domain. Only the reset provider is
+ * touched. The lease is put again, which never asserts; the next preparation
+ * takes its own. Caller holds owner/lifecycle exclusion.
  */
 int qcom_usb4_x1_release_retired_reset(struct device *owner)
 {
@@ -2190,8 +2192,8 @@ out:
 
 /* Omarchy flavor: release a PCIe0 preparation that never trained, allocated a
  * bridge or receiver, or took the receiver's module pin. Same provider order
- * as qcom_usb4_x1_retire_platform(); the tunnel BCR stays asserted for the
- * next generation's preparation.
+ * as qcom_usb4_x1_retire_platform(); the tunnel BCR is still asserted on
+ * return, and the caller releases it before the router's domain goes off.
  */
 int qcom_usb4_x1_idle_retire_platform(struct device *owner, struct x1_pcie_state *state,
 				      int (*check_stopped)(void *), void *context)
